>
  <div class="tls-article" id="top">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <div class="page-hero-inner">
        <span class="hero-eyebrow">Methods · Compaction Grouting &amp; Mud Jacking</span>
        <h1>Compaction Grouting &amp; Mud Jacking —<br /><em>Same Grout, Two Jobs</em></h1>
        <p class="page-hero-sub">Ground improvement by displacement, not a pile and not foam. One material, aimed at the soil itself or aimed under a slab, depending on which problem you actually have.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="article-outer">
      <article class="article-body">
        <p class="article-lede">"Grouting" gets used loosely enough on estimates that it stops meaning anything specific. This page is about the one mechanism that actually matters: a stiff, low-mobility grout that displaces the material around it instead of soaking into it.</p>

        <div class="article-section" id="what-they-are">
          <h2>What They <em>Actually Are</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Both compaction grouting and mud jacking use a stiff, low-slump, low-mobility grout — meant to stay near the injection point and form a bulb, or a string of bulbs, rather than soak into ordinary soil pores the way a thinner grout would.</p>
          <p>The difference is entirely about where it's aimed. Compaction grouting injects into the soil itself — as each bulb grows under controlled pressure, it displaces the ground around it, and in suitable material that displacement increases density and stiffness. Mud jacking uses the same material but aimed beneath a slab instead, filling a void to restore contact and lift the concrete. Same grout, two different targets, two different jobs.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="how-they-work">
          <h2>How They Actually <em>Work</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Neither of these creates a conventional pile that carries building load down to a distant deep layer — they're meant to improve or support what's already there, not replace it with something new.</p>
          <p>For compaction grouting, what you get depends on the soil itself, the treatment depths and pattern, the injection sequence, pressure and volume control, and monitoring throughout. It's ground improvement, not "adding concrete underground" as a slogan — the mechanism is displacement and densification of material that's already lost density.</p>
          <div class="callout-block">
            <span class="callout-label">Worth knowing</span>
            <p>Compaction grouting is a different tool from foam injection, even though both can be used to address a settled slab. Foam is an expanding polyurethane resin that fills a void; compaction grout is a stiff cement-based material that displaces and compacts the ground around it. They're not interchangeable, and a bid that just says "grouting" or "soil stabilization" hasn't actually named a method — ask what mechanism is planned.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="scope">
          <h2>What Rules Either One <em>Out</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">An active, unaddressed leak, roof runoff, grading, or irrigation problem still driving the movement.</p>
          <p>A foundation that actually needs a defined deep load path rather than improved shallow or intermediate support. Nearby foundations, utilities, and slabs that haven't been accounted for — displacement can move more than just the target zone.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="installation">
          <h2>What Installation <em>Actually Involves</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Both are injected through a grid of small-diameter holes at planned locations, depths, and stages, with volume and pressure controlled and monitored throughout — not a single injection point, and not an open-ended pour.</p>
          <p>[TIM: how often does this actually come up in your NM work, and what does a typical job look like when it does — equipment, timeline, anything homeowners should expect that's different from the piering jobs?]</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="misconceptions">
          <h2>What People <em>Get Wrong</em></h2>
          <ul class="key-points">
            <li><strong>"Grouting is grouting"</strong> — compaction grouting, permeation grouting, and mud jacking use different materials aimed at different mechanisms. A proposal that just says "grouting" hasn't named a method yet.</li>
            <li><strong>"This is the same as foam injection"</strong> — both can lift a slab, but the material and mechanism are different: cement-based displacement versus expanding polyurethane. Cost, cure time, and permanence differ accordingly.</li>
            <li><strong>"It's a substitute for piers"</strong> — it improves the ground or lifts a slab; it doesn't create a structural load path the way a pier does. If the foundation genuinely needs deep support, this isn't a substitute for that.</li>
            <li><strong>"More grout is always better"</strong> — uncontrolled volume can lift or fracture more than the target zone. Planned, monitored injection is the whole point.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>

        <p class="closing-link"><a href="/foundation-repair-methods" class="body-link">← Back to Foundation Repair Methods</a> · <a href="/glossary#compaction-grouting" class="body-link">Glossary: Compaction Grouting →</a></p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

